# The oracle images, pinned by digest.
#
# One line per oracle: <name> <image-ref> <version> <what it answers>
#
# The ref carries a tag *and* a digest. The tag is for a human reading this
# file; the digest is what runs. A tag can be moved under you and a digest
# cannot, so a green run names a set of bytes rather than a set of names.
#
# The version field is checked at run time against what the reference says it
# is, and for this library it names the **UCD version, not the interpreter's**.
# That is the pin that decides what a comparison *means*.
#
# It is not the pin that decides what the reference *answers*, and the first
# version of this file said it was - "CPython's own release number is
# incidental". It is not. CPython 3.13.5 and 3.13.15 both report
# `unidata_version` 15.1.0, and `unicodedata.decomposition()` returns nothing for
# a Hangul syllable on the first and `1100 1161` on the second: same table, two
# implementations of it. So the two pins here are not redundant and neither
# substitutes for the other - **the version field catches a drifted table and the
# digest catches a drifted implementation of the same table.** That is why every
# ref below carries both, and why host mode, which has neither, prints
# `unpinned`. `text` found this by measuring four interpreters; the exclusion
# it cost this differential is gone, in the commit that deleted it.
#
# Raising a pin is deliberate. A new reference version is new answers, and new
# answers that disagree are findings rather than regressions - see the triage
# in unicodedata_diff.py's header, every line of which is a Consortium decision
# rather than a defect.

# The gating pin: a released interpreter. UCD 16.0.0 against these tables'
# 17.0.0, so the differential is advisory here and its triage still applies -
# though it is already two entries shorter than it was against the 15.1 that
# this machine's python3 carries. U+1171E and U+226D agree with the library
# under 16.0; U+0295 and U+5146 do not yet.
python	docker.io/library/python:3.14-slim@sha256:7bf6c3111fe094f8ee1a1cbcdc63c4cfb345b0e3df42d5aa9a90b3b4b022ab6d	unicodedata 16.0.0	General_Category, Bidi_Class, East_Asian_Width, Canonical_Combining_Class, Bidi_Mirrored, Decomposition_Type, Numeric_Value, Name

# A second CPython, not a replacement. This one carries UCD 17.0.0, an exact
# match with tools/ucd/UCD_VERSION, which is what retires the not-comparable
# category outright: the DerivedAge filter, the property-changed-since caveat
# and the whole triage list exist only because the oracle is behind.
#
# It is a release candidate, which is why it is not the gating pin above - but
# it is the pin `check-oracle-unicodedata-strict` uses, because a matching UCD
# is the only configuration in which a disagreement is necessarily a defect.
# When 3.15.0 is released this becomes the gating pin and the line above it
# stays, for the reading between the two.
python-next	docker.io/library/python:3.15-rc-slim@sha256:4c269ae51995d15c7115f3403bde620e5aa5387ca5b865e56a61a4a24c9ffb49	unicodedata 17.0.0	The same questions at this library's own UCD version

# ICU, the only second opinion there is on segmentation: `unicodedata` exposes
# no boundary function at all, so UAX #29 and UAX #14 have had nothing but the
# Consortium's conformance files to answer for them.
#
# **Built here, not installed, and the version is why.** ICU carries a Unicode
# version of its own - Debian 13's ICU 76.1 is Unicode 16.0 - and 78.3 is
# Unicode 17.0, an exact match for tools/ucd/UCD_VERSION. 78.3 is in Debian sid
# and installing it from there was the cheap option and the wrong one: sid moves
# continuously, so an exact apt pin into it expires sooner than a stable one,
# and this is the one gate here whose disagreement means a defect. The source
# release has no expiry. See containers/icu/Dockerfile.
#
# Being built here, the pin is not a digest (two builds of one Dockerfile are
# not two copies of one image). What is pinned is everything the build reads:
# the base by digest and the tarball by the SHA-512 the Consortium publishes
# beside the release. The run-time version check below is the real guarantee.
#
# One pin rather than the two the CPython oracle keeps, because there is no
# released-versus-candidate tension: 78.3 is released *and* matches.
#
# **When this pin moves, run `make check-oracle-icu-exhaustive`.** A new ICU is
# new tailorings, and the explanation table in icu_break_diff.py names the ones
# measured under 78.3 - every codepoint, three contexts, all three LB1
# resolutions. The sampling gate would not necessarily notice a tailoring the
# table does not know about, and the explainer's guard suite certainly would not:
# its cases are the ones somebody thought of.
icu	localhost/ghoti-unicode-oracle-icu:78.3	Unicode 17.0	UAX #29 grapheme, word and sentence boundaries; UAX #14 line breaks under all three LB1 tailorings
